<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Korean War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1950">1950</a>–<a href="/years/1953">1953</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Korean War began on 25 June 1950 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> force led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> drove the North back towards the Chinese border, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> then intervened with massive forces. After the front stabilised near the 38th parallel, an armistice was signed in July 1953. The war turned occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> into a supply base and hardened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> divisions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/korean-war.jpg" alt="Korean War" width="320" height="257" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photo by Corporal Peter McDonald, USMC · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chosin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> had been a colony of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a> from 1910 to 1945. As the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> drew to a close, two American colonels, Dean Rusk and Charles H. Bonesteel III, chose the 38th parallel on 11 August 1945 as the line dividing the peninsula into a Soviet and an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/occupation-of-japan">American occupation</a> zone. The zones formed their own governments in 1948: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-il-sung">Kim Il Sung</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syngman-rhee">Syngman Rhee</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a>, each claiming to be the only legitimate government of all Korea. Border clashes were extensive, and Kim Il Sung began seeking Stalin's support for an invasion in March 1949.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 25 June 1950 the Korean People's Army crossed the 38th parallel behind artillery fire, with tanks against a South Korean army that had none. The UN Security Council denounced the invasion the same day; the Soviet representative, whose government was boycotting the council, was absent and could not veto. UN forces comprised 21 countries, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> providing around 90 per cent of the military personnel. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a> fell on 28 June, and by early August the South Korean army and its allies held only the Pusan Perimeter in the south-east.</p><p>On 15 September UN forces landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incheon">Incheon</a>, well behind the North Korean lines, and broke out of the perimeter soon afterwards. They retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a> and invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a> in October. On 19 October the Chinese People's Volunteer Army crossed the Yalu and entered the war. Chinese and North Korean forces took Seoul a second time on 4 January 1951, before a UN counter-offensive regained it two months later. The fighting then became a war of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attrition">attrition</a> with little change of territory, while armistice talks began on 10 July 1951 and were delayed for two years by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/repatriation-of-cultural-property">repatriation</a> of prisoners of war. Throughout, the United Nations Command bombed North Korea.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Combat ended on 27 July 1953 with the signing of the Korean Armistice Agreement, which allowed the exchange of prisoners and created a demilitarised zone four kilometres wide along the front line. The war caused around one million military deaths and an estimated 1.5 million to 3 million civilian deaths. Alleged war crimes include the mass killing of alleged communists by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>, the Seoul National University Hospital massacre by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a> and the carpet bombing of North Korea by the United Nations Command. No peace treaty has been signed. The war saw armoured offensives, tunnel warfare by Chinese forces and the first large jet aircraft battles.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Korean War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A founding member of NATO in 1949, Canada fought in the <b>Korean War</b>, joined the United States in the North American Air Defence Command in 1958, took part in many United Nations peacekeeping missions, and fought in Afghanistan from 2001.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canada">Canada · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The railway network linking Pusan, Seoul and the Chinese border tied Korea's economy to Japan and carried troops to the continent; most of the infrastructure built then was destroyed in the <b>Korean War</b> of 1950–1953.</blockquote><a href="/empire/korea-under-japanese-rule">Korea under Japanese rule · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…was a prosperous farming economy built on the export of wool, meat and dairy products to Britain, which in the early 1950s took around two-thirds of its exports; the wool boom during the <b>Korean War</b> briefly made it one of the richest countries per head in the world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In October 1950 Chinese People’s Volunteers under Peng Dehuai entered the <b>Korean War</b>, drove United Nations forces back from the Yalu River and fought them to a stalemate before the armistice of July 1953, at a cost of hundreds of thousands of Chinese dead.</blockquote><a href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1932–1933 Colombia fought Peru over the Amazon port of Leticia, which it kept after League of Nations mediation, and in 1951–1953 it sent an infantry battalion and frigates to the <b>Korean War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-colombia">Republic of Colombia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Turkey stayed neutral in almost all of the Second World War, declaring war on 23 February 1945, fought with the UN forces in the <b>Korean War</b> and joined NATO in 1952.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-turkey">Republic of Turkey · Military</a></li></ul></section>
